  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Evaluating on fewer than 4 benchmarks: Influence-guided context selection received uniformly low scores (all 4s at NeurIPS) partly due to limited evaluation scope with 36% OOD performance drops. LDAR's split reviews (2/4/6/8) also reflected evaluation concerns. Papers accepted at top venues in this area (Self-RAG at ICLR Oral, Provence at ICLR) evaluate on 6-7 benchmarks.",
    "Requiring task-specific retraining without addressing zero-shot transfer: all 4 reviewers of Influence-guided raised this as a fundamental limitation. LDAR's 'OOD corpus transfer unproven' was a main criticism. Any proposed method must either demonstrate zero-shot generalization or provide a compelling argument for why task-specific adaptation is acceptable.",
    "Processing passages independently while claiming to model set-level properties: Self-RAG's per-passage IsRel/IsSup tokens cannot detect cross-passage interference. CI value's additive leave-one-out decomposition ignores pairwise effects. If you claim set-level optimization, your method must actually model inter-passage interactions, not just aggregate independent scores.",
    "Using manually-tuned multi-component utility functions without principled calibration: AdaGATE's 5 manually-weighted components (λ₁-λ₅) and DynamicRAG's heuristically-weighted composite reward lack principled justification. Reviewers increasingly expect either formal derivation of weights (like AdaGReS's closed-form β*) or learned parameters (like InSQuAD's end-to-end SMI).",
    "Relying on proprietary LLM distillation for core training signals: SetR's GPT-4o distillation, Self-RAG's GPT-4 annotations, and Provence's Llama-3-8B silver labels were all flagged as reproducibility concerns. Use open-weight models for training signals and clearly state the dependency.",
    "Claiming distraction-awareness as novel without acknowledging LDAR and The Distracting Effect: both papers already establish distraction as a concept and provide measurement tools. The novelty must be in the formalization (separate optimization terms), the scope (post-retrieval composition vs. retrieval-time), or the theoretical properties (approximation guarantees), not in simply recognizing that distraction exists.",
    "Framing a contribution as 'adding a term to an existing objective' without demonstrating structural novelty in the resulting optimization landscape: the baseline fear test is real — if AdaGReS + distraction score as a simple reranking feature matches performance, the submodular formalization adds no value. Must show theoretically (e.g., non-monotonicity changes approximation analysis) or empirically (e.g., interaction effects between terms) that the extension is non-trivial.",
    "Ignoring computational cost analysis: RRPO's O(k·n) LLM calls during training and LDAR's 'training cost opacity' were explicitly criticized. Any method using LLM feedback must clearly state training FLOPs, inference latency, and total compute budget. For AAAI 2026 with a 200 H100-hour constraint, this is especially critical."
